---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';

/**
 * SEO / compatibility page (research Part 12 P7, spec section 4 of badger-fit
 * docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-18-m-web-4-orphaned-pages.md).
 *
 * TRADEMARK HYGIENE, binding: StrongLifts is someone else's mark. Everything on
 * this page is phrased as compatibility (Badger imports their CSV; Badger ships
 * a 5x5 template written for Badger), never as affiliation, partnership, or
 * endorsement. The closing note says so in words. Do not reword the title, the
 * h1, or the note into anything that reads as "the StrongLifts app for X".
 *
 * Every capability claim here was checked against the app source on 2026-07-25,
 * app version 1.1.1: the seeded template contents (app_database.dart
 * _seedProgramsV24), the suggestion-only progressive overload
 * (progressive_overload_provider.dart), the deload behavior (features/deload/),
 * plateau detection defaults, and the Stronglifts CSV parser's actual column
 * coverage (stronglifts_parser.dart). The "what Badger does not do" section is
 * the load-bearing half of the page: it is what makes the rest believable to
 * someone arriving from an app that did run the program for them.
 *
 * Re-checked for 1.1.12 on 2026-09-23: the seeded StrongLifts rules (+2.5 kg
 * per lift, +5 kg deadlift, -10% after three misses) and the 5x5 -> 3x5 -> 1x5
 * stall ladder (seed_programs.dart _seedProgramsV69Rules and
 * _seedProgramsV70StallLadder, new installs only; the gallery file carries
 * both), and re-importing duplicating overlapping sets (import_overlap.dart).
 */

const description =
  'Run a 5x5 in Badger and bring your StrongLifts history with you. What the built-in template does, what you still decide yourself, and exactly what the CSV import carries across.';
---

<HelpLayout title="StrongLifts 5x5 tracker" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="StrongLifts 5x5 in Badger"
    description={description}
    dateModified="2026-09-24"
    about={['StrongLifts 5x5', '5x5 strength training']}
  />

  <h1>StrongLifts 5x5 in Badger</h1>

  <p>
    Badger runs StrongLifts 5x5 for free and imports your StrongLifts history. It proposes each
    session's weight from the program's own rules, and you have the final say. This page covers the
    built-in template, what you still decide, and what the import brings across.
  </p>

  <h2 id="whats-in-the-box">What Badger brings to a 5x5</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The template ships with the app.</strong> Under More &rarr; Routines, in the Beginner
      Programs folder, the StrongLifts 5x5 routine has two alternating days. Workout A is squat, bench
      press and barbell row, five sets of five each. Workout B is squat and overhead press, five sets
      of five, plus one set of five deadlifts. Set it active and it is ready.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It alternates for you.</strong> The days are not pinned to weekdays, so the Today screen
      leads with whichever comes next.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You supply the weights.</strong> The template carries the rep scheme only. In your first
      session you enter what you lift; after that the rules propose each weight from the last session.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The program's rules come with it.</strong> Each lift carries the StrongLifts rule as a
      sentence: <em>After 3 misses, drop 10%. When all working sets hit the plan, add 2.5 kg.</em> The
      deadlift adds 5 kg. If you log in pounds, the steps read 5 lbs and 10 lbs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The stall ladder is built in.</strong> Three misses in a row on a lift also move it from
      5x5 to 3x5, then to 1x5. The deadlift is already 1x5 and stays there.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A rest timer that starts itself</strong> when you complete a working set: two minutes by
      default, and each exercise can have its own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A plate calculator</strong> that works from the plates your gym owns, in barbell,
      machine or single-lever mode, and shows what goes on each side.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A warmup generator</strong> that builds the ramp to your work weight, rounding every rung
      down to a load your plates can make.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>PRs and graphs on every lift</strong>, with no cap on routines or history.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-you-decide">What you still decide yourself</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Every weight is a proposal.</strong> When you start a session, the preview shows the
      weight the rule chose and the line that fired, for example <em>Rule met: all sets hit the plan
      last session</em>. Type your own weight and the rule stays on. The rule never edits the routine
      itself. See <a href="/help/progression/#rules">progression rules</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every number in the rule.</strong> Tap the increment, the percentage or the number of
      misses and type a new value.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Installed Badger before 1.1.12?</strong> Your copy of the template has no rules. Import
      <a href="/programs/">stronglifts-5x5.badger</a> to get the rules and the stall ladder, or use
      <a href="/help/progression/#set-up-progression">Set up progression</a> on your routine, which
      adds the rules without the ladder.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Whether to take a deload week.</strong> The app-wide deload is a separate feature, off by
      default. It schedules a light week at half the weight with two fewer working sets, and you
      confirm or decline it when you start that session. Both defaults are adjustable.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Two things Badger does not have: a watch app on either platform, and sync between devices. Your
    log lives on the phone you train with; an optional backup to your own Google Drive, or a database
    file you move yourself, carries it to a new one. See
    <a href="/help/phone-migration/">moving to a new phone</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="caps">The short version</span>
    <p>
      Badger holds the program, proposes each weight from its rules, times your rests and shows you the
      graph. Every proposal says why, and you can type over any of them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="import">Bringing your StrongLifts history over</h2>

  <p>
    In StrongLifts, go to <strong>Settings &rarr; Export workout data</strong> to get a CSV, move it
    to the phone you train with, then open <strong>Settings &rarr; Your data &rarr; Import from
    Stronglifts</strong> in Badger. Import each file once. The full steps are in
    <a href="/help/getting-started/#importing">importing from another app</a>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>What comes across:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Every set, with its date, exercise, weight and reps.</li>
    <li>Kilograms or pounds, read from the file's headers and kept in the unit you logged, with no rounding.</li>
    <li>The workout name, as a note on that session, and each row's note, on its first set.</li>
    <li>When each session started and how long it ran.</li>
    <li>
      Your PRs, worked out from the imported history. New exercises land in an "Imported" category
      for you to sort.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>What does not come across:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Warmups.</strong> The StrongLifts export does not mark them, so every set arrives as a
      working set.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Failed sets, as failures.</strong> A missed set of five comes in as the reps you did.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Which program a session belonged to.</strong> The import brings history; you set the
      5x5 template active separately.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="the-file">The template as a file you can read</h2>

  <p>
    The same 5x5 routine is a download on the <a href="/programs/">programs page</a>:
    <code>stronglifts-5x5.badger</code>, exported from the app. Open it on your phone and Badger
    imports it. Open it in a text editor to read or edit it first; the
    <a href="/badger-format/">file format</a> is documented.
  </p>

  <h2 id="after-5x5">When 5x5 runs out</h2>

  <p>
    Linear progression stalls for everyone, usually within months. Here is where to go next in Badger.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Stretch it first.</strong> The stall ladder does part of this for you. Smaller jumps, a
      light week and longer rests buy more time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Madcow 5x5</strong> is the usual next step once 1x5 stalls. It ships in the Intermediate
      Programs folder with weekly progression written in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>GZCLP, 5/3/1 BBB and nSuns 531LP</strong> ship too, with their rules. 5/3/1 and nSuns
      work from a <a href="/help/progression/#training-max">training max</a> that Badger keeps per lift
      and turns into loads your plates can make. All of them download from the
      <a href="/programs/">programs page</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="cost">What it costs</h2>

  <p>
    Nothing. Badger is free on Google Play and the App Store, with no account, no subscription, no ads
    and no cap on routines or history. <a href="/guides/workout-tracker-no-subscription/">The
    no-subscription guide</a> compares what free means across the trackers people usually weigh up.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trademark">About the name</h2>

  <p class="fineprint">
    StrongLifts is a trademark of its owner. Badger is not affiliated with, endorsed by, sponsored by,
    or connected to StrongLifts in any way. This page describes compatibility only: Badger reads the
    CSV file that the StrongLifts app exports, and the 5x5 routine inside Badger was written for
    Badger from the publicly known shape of the program.
  </p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  /* The trademark note is fine print by intent: it is a disclaimer, and it should
     read as one rather than competing with the guidance above it. Muted is
     AA-checked against the page ground in both themes, so it stays readable. */
  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
